language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ just_forgotten: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Joe forgot his account number. It has all the digits from 0 to n-1 in some order. He tried several sets of digits, and in each set exactly the same stated number of digits are in their correct position, that is, in the same position as in the account number. From these sets, find the account number.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `just_forgotten.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/just_forgotten.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `just_forgotten_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py just_forgotten.json
$   savilerow model.eprime just_forgotten_1.param -run-solver

$ number of sets tried, derived from the data (the reader's JSON has no such field)
given sets_dim1 : int
$ number of digits n, derived from the data: the length of each set (the reader's JSON has no such field)
given sets_dim2 : int
$ sets[k,p]: the digit at position p of the k-th set tried, positions and sets indexed from 1 (the data's index 0 is 1)
given sets : matrix indexed by [int(1..sets_dim1), int(1..sets_dim2)] of int
$ the number of digits of each set that are in their correct position
given num_correct_digits : int

$ n: the number of digits in the account number
letting n = sets_dim2

$ x[p]: the digit at position p of the account number (positions indexed from 1 here; the output lists them from position 0)
find x : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of int(0..n-1)

such that
$ the account number has each of the digits from 0 to n-1 exactly once
allDiff(x),
$ each set tried has exactly num_correct_digits digits in the same position as the account number
forAll k : int(1..sets_dim1) . ((sum p : int(1..n) . toInt(x[p] = sets[k,p])) = num_correct_digits)
